+With the rapid advancement of the Internet and t
+he widespread adoption of mobile devices, the amo
+unt of available information and content has increa
+sed dramatically. In particular, as markets in vario
+us service domains such as e-commerce, streamin
+g services, and social media continue to expand, th
+e overwhelming abundance of products and content 
+has made it increasingly difficult for users to identi
+fy items that best match their preferences. In respo
+nse to this challenge, recommender systems have e
+merged as a technology that assists users in makin
+g appropriate choices. A recommender system is a 
+type of Information Filtering technology that aims t
+o improve user satisfaction by recommending infor
+mation that users are likely to find interesting [1][2]
+[3][4]. Therefore, recommender systems contribute 
+to alleviating the problem of information overload 
+while providing significant benefits to both users an
+d service providers.
+The two most important factors in recommender 
+systems are training speed and recommendation ac
+curacy. Training speed refers to how quickly a reco
+mmender system can learn from newly acquired da
+ta and provide users with appropriate recommenda
+tions in real time. Since user preferences and beha
+vioral patterns change rapidly over time, a recomm
+ender system that fails to promptly reflect these ch
+anges may provide outdated recommendations, ther
+eby reducing user satisfaction. In addition, recomm
+endation accuracy is an important metric that indic
+ates how relevant and appropriate the recommende
+d items are for individual users. A highly accurate 
+recommender system effectively captures user pref
+erences and delivers personalized content [5][6][7]
+[8][9][10].
+To address these issues, this study proposes a n
+ovel method that improves the performance of a Gr
+aph Signal Processing (GSP)-based recommender sy
+stem by incorporating user rating information. Con
+ventional Graph Convolution Network (GCN)-based 
+recommender systems effectively model complex gr
+aph structures through graph convolution operatio
+ns. However, they require a large number of traina
+ble parameters, and the optimization process involv
+es substantial computational cost and long training 
+time [12][13][14][15][16]. As a result, updating the re
+commender system model is time-consuming. To o
+vercome this limitation, Graph Signal Processing-b
+ased recommender systems have been introduced a
+s an efficient alternative for modeling relatively sim
+ple relationships [17][18][19][20][21][22].
+Graph Signal Processing-based recommender sys
+tems perform recommendation through a low-pass 
+filter, which consists of simple matrix operations an
+d does not require the learning of trainable weight 
+parameters. Consequently, the model can be updat
+ed efficiently with significantly lower computational 
+overhead. Furthermore, their recommendation acc
+uracy is comparable to or even higher than that of 
+Graph Convolution Network-based recommender sy
+stems [19][20][21][22].
+However, existing Graph Signal Processing-based 
+recommender systems treat items with high user ra
+tings and those with low user ratings equally witho
+ut distinguishing between them when constructing t
+he model [22]. Therefore, this study proposes a met
+hod that incorporates users' high rating informatio
+n into the model to achieve more personalized reco
+mmendations.
