-tracting item features and its inability to fully captu
-re users' diverse interests.
-Collaborative Filtering is based on the assumptio
-n that users with similar preferences tend to favor 
-similar items [3][5][6][8][9][10][11]. It is categorized i
-nto User-Based and Item-Based Collaborative Filteri
-ng [7]. This approach does not require item feature 
-information and can effectively discover users' pote
-ntial interests. However, it suffers from the Cold St
-art Problem, the Data Sparsity Problem, and increa
-sing computational complexity as user–item interac
-tions grow.
-Hybrid Filtering combines Content-Based and Col
-laborative Filtering to improve recommendation per
-formance. It can alleviate the Cold Start Problem w
-hile enhancing recommendation diversity, although 
-it introduces higher model complexity and computa
-tional cost.
-With the advancement of deep learning-based Co
-llaborative Filtering, Graph Convolution Networks 
-(GCNs) have been widely applied to recommender s
-ystems [12]. Representative models include NGCF [1
-3], LightGCN [14], SimGCL [15], and XSimGCL [16].
-Graph Signal Processing (GSP) extends conventio
-nal signal processing to graph-structured data by e
-xploiting graph topology [17][18]. Its core concepts i
-nclude Graph Signals, the Graph Laplacian Matrix, 
-Graph Filtering, and the Graph Fourier Transform 
-(GFT). In particular, Graph Filtering transforms gra
-ph signals into the frequency domain, where Low-p
-ass Filters suppress high-frequency components w
-hile preserving the similarities among neighboring 
-nodes.
-GF-CF (Graph Filter-based Collaborative Filterin
-g) is a representative recommender system based o
-n Graph Signal Processing [19]. To capture user–ite
-m similarities, GF-CF employs both Linear Low-pas
-s and Ideal Low-pass Filters. Subsequently, several 
-improved models were proposed, including PGSP, w
-hich incorporates richer user information [20], Tur
-bo-CF, which improves computational efficiency [2
-1], and HiGSP, which introduces hierarchical user c
-lustering before graph filtering [22].
-This study is based on HiGSP, which achieved sta
-te-of-the-art performance among Graph Signal Pro
-cessing-based recommender systems as of 2024. Hi
-GSP combines a cluster-based filtering module with 
-a global-aware filtering module consisting of an Ide
-al Low-pass Filter and a High-order Low-pass Filte
-r to effectively model user preferences and predict 
-future interactions.
-A previous study [19] compared the Graph Convo
-lution Network-based model LightGCN with the Gra
-ph Signal Processing-based model GF-CF. Since GF
--CF operates solely through matrix operations with
-out trainable parameters, it achieves significantly h
-igher computational efficiency while maintaining co
-mparable or even superior recommendation perfor
-mance on the Yelp2018 and Amazon-book datasets. 
-Motivated by these advantages, this study proposes 
-a method that further improves recommendation p
-erformance while preserving the computational effi
-ciency of Graph Signal Processing-based recomme
-nder systems.
-III. The Proposed Scheme
-Existing Graph Signal Processing-based recomme
-nder systems, including HiGSP, construct the User I
-nteraction Matrix for building the adjacency matrix 
-by considering only whether an interaction exists b
-etween a user and an item, without taking user rati
-ng information into account [19][20][21][22]. This ap
-proach is based on the assumption that users inter
-act more frequently with items they prefer. Howeve
-r, in real-world scenarios, users may repeatedly int
-eract with items to which they assign low ratings, w
-hile interacting less frequently with items they rate 
-highly. Consequently, an adjacency matrix construc
-ted solely from interaction information may fail to 
-adequately reflect users' actual preferences, potenti
-ally leading to degraded recommendation performa
-nce.
-To address this limitation, this study proposes a 
-
-=== 4쪽 ===
-4   Performance Improvement of Recommendation Systems using User Evaluation Information
-method that additionally incorporates user rating in
-formation during the construction of the User Inter
-action Matrix. Specifically, a user–item connection i
-s created only when the rating assigned by the user 
-is greater than or equal to a predefined threshold 
-(N) (3 in this study), thereby constructing a new rat
-ing-based adjacency matrix. This approach aims to 
-strengthen the signals of users' preferred items an
-d improve personalized recommendation performan
-ce.
-However, using only the rating-based adjacency 
-matrix may result in information loss because inter
-actions associated with low ratings are excluded. T
-o overcome this issue, this study utilizes both the c
-onventional interaction-based adjacency matrix and 
-the proposed rating-based adjacency matrix. Graph 
-filtering is independently performed on each adjace
-ncy matrix, and the resulting representations are c
-ombined using a weighted aggregation strategy. Thi
-s approach effectively incorporates user preference 
-information while minimizing information loss [17][1
-8]. Furthermore, the proposed combination framew
-ork provides an ensemble effect. In Chapter 5, the 
-recommendation performance under different thres
-hold values of (N) is compared with that of existing 
-models through comprehensive experiments.
-Figure 1. Architecture of the Proposed Model
-Figure 1 illustrates the overall architecture of the 
-proposed model. The framework consists of two ma
-in components. The lower branch performs graph f
-iltering in the same manner as the original HiGSP 
-model, while the upper branch performs graph filte
-ring using the newly constructed adjacency matrix 
-proposed in this study [22]. By combining these two 
-branches, the proposed model alleviates the inform
-ation loss problem while achieving more personaliz
-ed recommendations and benefiting from an ensem
-ble effect.
-The lower branch of Figure 1 constructs the adja
-cency matrix using the same procedure as the origi
-nal HiGSP model to prevent information loss cause
-d by the proposed method. After constructing the a
-djacency matrix, clustering is performed to group s
-imilar users, followed by the application of a Linear 
-Low-pass Filter to compute the rating scores. Subs
-equently, the original adjacency matrix is processe
-d using an Ideal Low-pass Filter and a High-order 
-Low-pass Filter, respectively, to generate additional 
-rating scores [22]. Through the application of multi
-ple Low-pass Filters, the model effectively captures 
-user preferences and improves recommendation qu
-ality.
-The upper branch of Figure 1 constructs a new a
-djacency matrix using the proposed method and pe
-rforms graph filtering based on this newly generate
-d graph structure. Similar to the lower branch, clu
-stering is first applied to group users with similar p
-references, followed by a Linear Low-pass Filter to 
-compute rating scores. The proposed adjacency ma
-trix is then processed using both an Ideal Low-pass 
-Filter and a High-order Low-pass Filter to generate 
-rating scores. Unlike conventional approaches that 
-rely solely on interaction information, the proposed 
-method incorporates users' high rating information 
-into the graph filtering process, enabling more acc
-urate and personalized recommendations. In the fin
-al stage, the four rating matrices generated from th
-e above procedures are combined using weighted a
-ggregation to produce the final rating matrix. Thro
-ugh this framework, the proposed model addresses 
-the limitations of the original HiGSP while simultane
-ously benefiting from an ensemble effect, thereby i
-mproving recommendation performance.
